extra(100,'unproved claim',16,[3,4],[27,122],'“E=∑E^(ν)”; “π_i”','Off-topic algebra: orthogonal idempotents summing to1 define the grading E^(ν)=Σπ_(i+ν)Eπ_i; products have degreeν+ν′, and π_i are central in E^(0).','The first displayed minus sign is a mathematical error if retained; centrality is not asserted in all of E.',[29])
claim(100,[100],'Orthogonal projector decomposition, c16:3–4.','A finite orthogonal family π_i with sum1 defines a direct grading by matrix diagonals; E^(ν)E^(ν′)⊂E^(ν+ν′). Each π_i is central in degree0, generally not in E.','algebraic preliminaries','Expand x=(Σπ_i)x(Σπ_j); orthogonality gives the sum-degree rule. A matrix unit e12 fails to commute with π1.','E','Elementary ring decomposition, verified directly; later Lefschetz expositions use this structure.','known algebra; checked2026-11-19','Valid with plus degree and degree0 centrality.',['Kleiman1968','AkhtarJoshua'],group='normalized')
extra(101,'unproved claim',16,[5,6,7,8],[123,264],'“les π_i sont déterminés”','Off-topic algebra: annihilator conditions and the inverse-pair relations recover the grading projectors inside a subring when the stated joint injectivity/generation hypotheses hold.','This claim concerns the already graded ring; it does not contradict the later L-alone nonuniqueness.',[29])
claim(101,[101],'Annihilator criteria(3.1),(3.2), inverse pairs(4.4), and descent from E⊂F.','Under the displayed annihilator hypotheses, the projectors are uniquely determined. If the inverse pairs and L lie in a graded subring, the endpoint projectors and then all projectors lie there by induction.','proof sketched in preliminaries','Uniqueness: each difference of candidate projectors annihilates the prescribed generating pieces, hence is zero. For descent, compose inverse pairs to recover the extreme projector, subtract it and induct.','D','Formal argument checked with the actual hypotheses; no exact separate publication of this integral ring formulation located.','2026-11-19','Conditional theorem; omitting the grading or annihilator condition is unjustified.',['Kleiman1968','AkhtarJoshua'],group='normalized')
extra(102,'self-flagged step',16,[9,10,11,12,13],[265,481],'“Démonstration à fournir”','Off-topic algebra: existence of two-sided graded hard-Lefschetz inverses is equivalent to the primitive decomposition and normalized lowering operator; one implication is expressly left to prove.','The reconstruction uses split finite strings and all grading projectors; the blank formula(6.5) is not filled diplomatically.',[2,29,31])
claim(102,[102],'Equivalence of inverse, lowering and primitive-projector conditions; deferred(iv)⇒(ii).','Given a graded hard-Lefschetz object, primitive kernels split the strings, defining unit lowering; conversely powers of lowering give the inverse on each relevant degree, and string projectors are recovered from endpoints.','one implication explicitly deferred','Check on L^jP^i with 0≤j≤n−i. The two equations in(6.3) are both needed. Matrix computations verify finite models, and the split-string argument is general.','A','Kleiman’s normalized Lefschetz formalism supplies a later complete treatment; the integral exterior model still fails separately.','1968 comparator','Qualified abstract equivalence valid; no inference that the raw symplectic contraction has unit coefficients.',['Kleiman1968'],group='normalized')
extra(103,'unproved claim',16,[18],[693,724],'“anneau opposé”','Off-topic algebra: passing to the opposite ring reverses composition and, with degree reversal, exchanges the raising/lowering and endpoint formulas.','This is a formal duality, not a statement that the ungraded pair alone recovers the grading.',[29])
claim(103,[103],'Opposite-ring symmetry of the Lefschetz formulas.','Apply E↦E^op and π_i↦π_(2n−i); reverse the order of every product. Two-sided inverse equations and endpoint identities transform into their dual partners.','asserted formal symmetry','Direct reversal of products verifies the formulas; neither a sign nor an anti-linear operation is needed.','E','Elementary formal duality, used in later Lefschetz treatments.','checked2026-11-19','Valid with simultaneous degree reversal.',['Kleiman1968'],group='normalized')
extra(104,'withdrawn in the same text',16,[20,22],[780,859,898,944],'“Non”','Off-topic algebra: equality of top powers does not force a second operator to satisfy hard Lefschetz; the author works out a counterexample.','The finite commutative graded ring and matrices are reconstructed explicitly in computations.py; retain the author’s negative conclusion.')
claim(104,[104],'Attempted converse from L′^n=L^n, rejected on c16:20,22.','Two degree2 operators can have the same nonzero top cube while one fails the middle Lefschetz isomorphism.','author supplies counterexample','same_top_power_not_lefschetz: middle matrix of L isI2, ofL′ is[[0,0],[1,1]]; determinants1 and0, equal nonzero top cubes.','D','Author’s rejection independently verified; no exact later publication located.','manuscript already negative; checked2026-11-19','Settled negative statement, not an open conjecture.',['Kleiman1968'],group='normalized')
extra(105,'unproved claim',16,[21,23],[860,977],'“orthogonal”; “non dégénérée”','Off-topic Brauer surface text: VℓBr is the rational orthogonal complement of NS, of dimension b2−ρ. Integral unimodularity additionally depends on the ℓ-part of the Néron–Severi discriminant.','A rational orthogonal decomposition is not automatically a direct integral unimodular decomposition.',[8])
claim(105,[105],'Rational and integral surface intersection comparison.','For a smooth proper connected surface over an algebraically closed field, ℓ invertible, the rational quotient H²/NS identifies with NS⊥. Its integral complement is unimodular precisely when the relevant NS discriminant is an ℓ-adic unit, after removing torsion and assuming the usual primitive embedding.','rational theorem plus integral qualification','Lattice discriminant calculation separates nondegeneracy overQℓ from determinant a unit overZℓ.','A','BrauerIII§8.2–8.3 gives the rational comparison; the integral assertion requires its lattice qualifications.','1966 preprint/1968 publication','No unrestricted integral orthogonal splitting.',['BrauerIII','Mumford1961'],group='brauer')
extra(106,'unproved claim',16,[21,23],[860,977],'“partie de torsion”; “Br′”','Off-topic Brauer text: the ℓ-primary torsion of NS matches torsion in H²(Zℓ(1)); the finite quotient of Br′(ℓ) by its divisible part corresponds to torsion in H³(Zℓ(1)).','The ℓ-primary qualifier is essential; the displayed unqualified Brauer quotient is not silently accepted.',[8])
claim(106,[106],'Torsion identifications from Kummer limits.','With the finiteness assumptions of BrauerIII§8.2, tors(NS)_(ℓ)=tors H²(Zℓ(1)) and Br′(ℓ)/Br′(ℓ)_div≅tors H³(Zℓ(1)).','asserted through exact limits','Use0→Zℓ(1)→Qℓ(1)→Qℓ/Zℓ(1)→0 and the Kummer sequence; the image from rational cohomology is divisible, leaving the next-degree torsion.','A','Published in BrauerIII§8.2; exact ℓ-primary convention retained.','1966/1968','Valid in finite cohomology setting; not a statement about all primes at once.',['BrauerIII','Stacks03PK'],group='brauer')
extra(107,'other',16,[35],[1365,1391],'“Cohomologie des catégories de Banach par Max Karoubi”','Off-topic reused leaf: Karoubi defines K(C) and proposes groups K^n(C) with Bott periodicity properties.','The opened1966 definitions note matches title, author and initial paragraph. It is not attributed to Grothendieck.')
claim(107,[107],'Karoubi’s K^n programme in the reused typescript.','The title and opening definitions match Karoubi’s C.R. note, Cohomologie des catégories de Banach: définitions. The surviving leaf itself states a programme and begins the axioms; it contains no Bott-periodicity proof.','reused named-author text','Visual comparison of printed first page with c16:35; no theorem beyond surviving text imputed.','E','Published by Karoubi in1966; applications note also opened.','definitions12September1966; applications5September1966, literal printed dates','Authorship resolved; mathematical programme belongs to Karoubi.',['Karoubi1966','Karoubi1966App'],group='karoubi')
extra(108,'unproved claim',16,[28,30],[1156,1187,1207,1236],'“H^r(A,G)=(0) for r>2”','Off-topic reused English text invokes Shatz’s duality and Tate to prove a vanishing theorem, but its A,G and local-field assumptions are absent from this fragment.','Bibliography says Shatz’s1964 article “to appear”; this dates a lead and does not identify the fragment’s author.')
claim(108,[108],'Vanishing H^r(A,G)=0 forr>2.','The argument reduces from A to a local field k using missing propositions14 and1, then uses a finite-group-scheme duality and Tate’s theorem. Without those antecedents no unique theorem statement can be recovered.','proof fragment beginning mid-theorem','The displayed steps only imply the stated vanishing under the missing reduction hypotheses; no arbitrary-ring theorem inferred.','F','Shatz1964 bibliography located but full text inaccessible; searches did not identify the reused English paper.','1964 cited article publication; fragment date uncertain','Unidentified claim, not classified false or solved merely by Shatz’s title.',['Shatz1964'],confidence='low',group='english')
extra(109,'unproved claim',16,[30],[1221,1235],'“Proposition15. For each s>0, the complex C(s) is acyclic”','Off-topic reused English text: for a purely inseparable field extensionK/k, a complex of H^s(K^{⊗r},Gm) built from face maps is asserted acyclic.','The cohomology topology and augmentation convention are not supplied locally; these matter for identifying Amitsur comparison results.')
claim(109,[109],'Acyclicity of C(s), s>0, for a purely inseparable extension.','The page asserts acyclicity of the cosimplicial cohomology-row complex. It must not be replaced by an assertion that all individual H^s vanish or that the Brauer group of k vanishes.','unproved proposition at end of fragment','Face-map complex definition read; no false reduction to ordinary Galois cohomology. Rosenberg–Zelinsky/Pareigis–Rosenberg lead located, source open returned429.','F','Relevant Amitsur literature located but exact proposition and topology not verified.','1962/1964 literature leads; search2026-11-19','Full paper or antecedent pages required for exact fate.',['Shatz1964'],confidence='low',group='english')
extra(110,'unproved claim',47,[3,4,6],[2086,2226,2280,2332],'“appli. classifiante”','A rigidified line kernel fibrewise inPic0 is classified by a mapX→A∨, and its transform is derived pullback of the universal Fourier transform.','Require the Picard functor hypotheses and use derived pullback for a nonflat classifying map.',[44,52])
claim(110,[110],'Classifying map and base change of a line-kernel transform.','Under representability of the dual abelian scheme, the rigidified kernel is the pullback of Poincaré. Proper perfect pushforward and derived base change identify Φ_L with Lψ*Φ_P.','stated and used','SubstituteL=(1×ψ)*P into pull–tensor–push, apply derived Cartesian base change.','B','Laumon1.2.2 and modern perfect-base-change criterion give the precise formula.','1996 preprint; current Stacks criterion','Valid derived statement; ordinary pullback only with appropriate Tor independence.',['Laumon1996','Stacks0DJT'],group='fourier')
extra(111,'unproved claim',47,[5,8,9],[2227,2278,2396,2471],'“rang”; “χ”; “−χ”','Fourier rank/Euler exchange and the determinant-induced homomorphism relation are proposed along with the nondegenerate line-bundle transform.','Ranks of derived objects are virtual; for a nonample line bundle use the WIT index and absolute Euler characteristic of its transformed sheaf.',[54])
claim(111,[111],'Rank, Euler characteristic and determinant relation.','Over a field, virtual rankΦ(F)=χ(F) andχ(Φ(F))=(−1)^g rankF. For ampleL, determinant ofφ_L*E=(L⁻¹)^⊕r givesφ_L*detE=L^(−r), hence the associated dual homomorphism composes withφ_L as−r·id after the stated identifications.','asserted consequences','Derived fibre at unit computesχ; apply inversion for second equality. Determinant of the verified pullback formula gives the isogeny relation, without choosing a fractional line bundle.','B','Mukai’s inversion and Proposition3.11 establish the field/ample cases.','1981','Relative and nonample statements need determinant line and WIT qualifications.',['Mukai1981','Laumon1996'],group='fourierchern')
extra(112,'unproved claim',47,[4,8],[2157,2226,2380,2419],'“automorphisme”; “symétrie”','The Poincaré transform is equivariant for automorphisms through the contragredient automorphism of the dual and commutes with inversion in the expected variance.','The classifying identity determines whether pullback or inverse pullback occurs; no unspecified equivariance convention is imposed.',[44,53])
claim(112,[112],'Automorphism equivariance of Fourier transform.','Foru:A→A an automorphism and dualu∨, normalized Poincaré gives(u×1)*P=(1×u∨)*P. Base change yields the corresponding contragredient pullback/pushforward identities; takingu=−1 gives inversion compatibility.','formal assertion','Kernel identity plus change of variables; exact variance follows from whetheru* oru_* is used.','B','Mukai§3 and Laumon functoriality provide the modern statement.','1981/1996','Valid after fixing variance; not an additional mysterious symmetry.',['Mukai1981','Laumon1996'],group='fourier')
extra(113,'conjecture',44,[4],[2868,2886],'“Th.6”; “noyau fini” cancelled','Off-topic intermediate-Jacobian programme gives weak-Lefschetz isomorphism, fixed-part and surjectivity ranges forP^i; one finite-kernel assertion is cancelled.','The P^i functor is not defined securely enough to equate it with an arbitrary modern algebraic representative.',[72,73,77])
claim(113,[113],'Weak-Lefschetz claims for the undefined P^i.','The source proposes isomorphism for2i≤n−1, a fixed factor at2i=n, and surjectivity for2i≥n+1, with a cancelled finite-kernel clause.','theorem list without proof or complete definitions','Degree ranges transcribed; modern weak Lefschetz on cohomology alone does not prove these functor-level assertions.','F','Related Picard/Albanese and codimension2 theories exist, but exact all-i claim cannot be matched without P^i.','searchthrough2026-11-19','Record as an underdefined programme, with cancellation retained.',['PicardVI1962','ACMV2023'],confidence='low',group='jacobian')
extra(114,'conjecture',44,[8],[2977,2984],'“H^i(Ω^{i−1})”','Off-topic letter proposes a tangent-space relationship between the intermediateJ^i and H^i(X,Ω_X^(i−1)).','The comparison cannot be asserted for an unknown equivalence relation definingJ^i; tangent spaces of Chow-type functors and abelian representatives differ.',[72])
claim(114,[114],'Proposed tangent-space description of J^i.','The suggested Hodge group is a candidate ambient tangent space, not an established canonical equality for an unspecified algebraic representative.','hope in a letter','Dimension/type comparison only; no equality proof claimed. EndpointPicard case works, but higher-i extrapolation needs a defined functor and differential.','F','No exact all-codimension theorem matching this draft formulation located.','search2026-11-19','Retain as a problem of defining the comparison map.',['PicardVI1962','ACMV2023'],confidence='low',group='jacobiantangent')
extra(115,'withdrawn in the same text',44,[5],[2891,2915],'“chaque α est indivisible dans P”','Off-topic cancelled root draft: reduced finite root vectors spanning a lattice and permuted by their reflections need not be primitive and need not have integral coroots.','CounterexampleΔ={±3e1,±3e2,±(e1+e2),±(e1−e2)}⊂Z²; all reflections preserveΔ but the coroot of3e1 pairs to2/3 with e1+e2. This is a new review computation, not a changed reading.')
claim(115,[115],'Claim that axioms(a)–(c) imply primitive roots and integral coroots.','The inference is false for the cancelled axioms as written. Reducedness and lattice stability under reflections do not impose the crystallographic integrality condition on each chosen root.','whole paragraph cancelled','root_counterexample.py verifies all8 reflections, reducedness, lattice generation, invariant form22I2 and nonintegral pairing2/3.','D','Literal implication refuted independently; modern root-system definitions impose integrality separately, as Conrad§1.3 explicitly does.','2026-11-19','Add primitivity/integrality rather than infer it from these axioms.',['Conrad2014','Milne2018'],group='rootcounter')
extra(116,'withdrawn in the same text',44,[5],[2901,2905,2918,2935],'“unique symétrie euclidienne”; “accouplés à Z”','Off-topic cancelled root draft: invariant positive form proves uniqueness of the reflection; the paired-lattice variant only has rational perfect duality and is not automatically a modern root datum.','For rank1 lattices withpairing2, rational duality androot reflections hold but the integral pairing is not perfect.')
claim(116,[116],'Reflection uniqueness and paired-lattice formulation.','Reflection uniqueness holds for a finite spanning root set even without integrality: two candidate reflections differ by a finite-order unipotent map, hence identity overQ. A rationally nondegenerate integral pairing need not be perfect overZ.','cancelled draft, two separate assertions','Conrad§1.3 gives the uniqueness proof; rank1 pairing[e,e′]=2 has cokernelZ/2 and demonstrates the lattice qualification.','E','Uniqueness is standard; modern root data require perfect integral duality, not just the weaker paired condition.','Conrad2014 verified comparator; elementary check2026','Do not call the entire cancelled construction a correct modern root-datum definition.',['Conrad2014','Milne2018'],group='groups')
extra(117,'unproved claim',16,[54,56],[1954,1995,2004,2049],'“étale”; “normal”; “réduit”','Off-topic étale typescript states preservation/descent of normality and reducedness and splitting of finite étale covers after a suitable finite faithfully flat base change.','Finite presentation, étaleness and the stated connected/rank conditions are retained; no arbitrary finite-algebra generalization.',[17,22])
claim(117,[117],'Étale preservation and finite-cover splitting.','Étale maps preserve normality and reducedness; these properties descend under faithfully flat maps. A finite étale algebra of constant rank splits after the finite étale cover parametrizing ordered geometric-fibre points.','stated in reused exposition','The ordering cover is a finite étale torsor underS_n when rankn is constant, giving a product algebra after base change. Nilpotent lifting handled separately.','A','Standard EGA/SGA étale theory; exact galley-to-publication match not claimed.','1960s theory; current verification2026','Qualified statements valid; the finite-fibre-only converse remains false.',['Stacks0BQB','EGAIV4'],group='etale')
extra(118,'reading question',54,[6],[2705,2710],'“I”; “plat”','Reused marginal ideal-adic flatness criterion is too compressed to recover its precise completeness, separation and Tor hypotheses.','It is not normalized into a modern local flatness criterion without missing conditions.',[70])
claim(118,[118],'Ideal-adic criterion in a reused margin.','The proposed relation between reductions modulo powers ofI and flatness requires completeness/separation or a suitable local finite-presentation hypothesis. The surviving notation does not identify which criterion is intended.','compressed marginal claim','No complete theorem reconstructed; generic freeness does not imply this criterion.','F','Relevant flatness literature searched; exact formulation unidentified.','search2026-11-19','Keep as a reading/context problem rather than a solved claim.',['Stacks051R','Stacks05P7'],confidence='low',group='flattening')
extra(119,'conjecture',16,[29],[1201,1204],'“donne n’importe quelle φ voulue…au facteur scalaire”','Off-topic Hodge model: nondegenerateQχ identifiesξ with a linear functional onΛ²V, but normalizingQ(ξ,ξ)=1 restricts possibleφ by nonisotropy and square classes.','A scalar normalization may require extending the field; the source’s phrase “up to scalar” is not a proof over every field.',[82,83,84])
claim(119,[119],'Choice ofξ produces any desired alternatingφ up to scalar.','Invertibility ofQχ gives a uniqueξ for eachφ as a linear functional, but the extra conditionQ(ξ,ξ)=1 is possible by scaling only whenQ(ξ,ξ)≠0 and its square class permits it.','assertion with scalar qualification','IfQ(ξ,ξ)=0, no nonzero rescaling makes it1; overQ a nonsquare norm has a further obstruction.','C','Linear part proved directly; unrestricted normalized claim requires the stated qualifications. No exact later model development located.','checked2026-11-19','Do not conflate linear surjectivity with normalized admissibility.',['Kleiman1968'],group='hodgealgebra')
extra(120,'reading question',16,[17],[650,679],'“π_(2n−i)”','Off-topic operator criterion on scan17 has a confirmed wrong projector in one of the inverse equations.','Use the two-sided equations on scan9 as an explicitly labelled mathematical repair, preserving current second-pass ink.',[29])
claim(120,[120],'The displayed inverse equation withπ_(2n−i),c16:17.','The right endpoint projector in the written product has the wrong degree for the asserted annihilation; the correct two-sided inverse identities are those of(6.3).','ink confirmed, mathematical error','scan17_wrong_projector: written matrixdiag(0,−1) is nonzero; corrected expression iszero.','D','Exact literal equation refuted; the abstract criterion survives with the corrected projector.','2026-11-19','A mathematical error is not silently treated as a transcription error.',['Kleiman1968'],group='normalized')
